  "language": "python",
  "case": "package_reexport",
  "root": ".repo",
  "why": "B20. Every caller reaches its target through a re-export and never through the defining module: the package `__init__` (`from .store import Store`), a rename (`helper as assist`), a two-link chain (`app` -> `app.sub` -> `app.sub.deep`), a star import with a literal `__all__` and one without, an ordinary module re-exporting what it imported (`app.relay`), the attribute form (`import app; app.Store()`), a member of a re-exported class (`Engine.start(...)`), and a base class inherited through the package. Python binds every imported name in the importing module, so each of these reaches the defining symbol at run time. Before the fix every one of these CALLS/IMPLEMENTS edges landed on an external placeholder named for the import text (`py:app.Store`), and IMPORTS stopped at the package.",
